WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
Defensive deallocation patterns make cleanup behavior boring and reviewable.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.860
Start by initializing ownership fields before use.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:15.760
A null pointer, count value, state flag, or ownership marker should have a defined meaning before any cleanup path can observe it.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:19.260
That helps failure paths release only what was actually acquired.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.880
Separate owned data from borrowed data.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.260
If a structure stores both, naming and comments should make the difference visible.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.880
For example, an owned copy has different cleanup behavior from a borrowed view.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.260
A destroy function should not have to guess which case applies for a field.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.480
Destroy behavior should be consistent.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:45.300
A destroy function should document what it frees, what it leaves alone, what state it expects, and whether the caller may pass a null pointer.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.960
If repeated calls are valid for a particular cleanup helper, say so and implement that behavior deliberately.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.380
If repeated calls are not valid, document that too.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.080
Avoid hidden frees and surprising side effects.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.820
A function that sounds like it only removes an item from a container should document whether it also destroys the item.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:10.400
A setter that replaces a field should document whether it frees the previous value, borrows the new value, or takes ownership of the new value.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.180
The strongest pattern is explicitness.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:19.880
Cleanup helpers, state transitions, and ownership names should make it hard for one allocation to be released from two authorities.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.860
Defensive deallocation is less about clever code and more about code whose cleanup story is easy to verify.
